WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.325
Lesson 1.0.

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:06.315
The Mastery Starter for Module 1: Foundations and Safety.

00:00:06.315 --> 00:00:08.119
By the end, you'll know the five

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:10.251
words this module runs on, and exactly

00:00:10.251 --> 00:00:12.219
how to tell when you've mastered it.

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:14.809
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:18.609
First, the sixty-second version: why almost every loss in

00:00:18.609 --> 00:00:21.464
crypto is a mistake, not a market move.

00:00:21.464 --> 00:00:23.141
Second, the five words you'll

00:00:23.141 --> 00:00:24.839
need, each one drawn out.

00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:27.254
Third, what to have ready before you start.

00:00:27.254 --> 00:00:30.519
Fourth, your first safe step: auditing your approvals.

00:00:30.519 --> 00:00:33.034
And finally, the mastery ladder, so you know

00:00:33.034 --> 00:00:35.604
exactly what finishing this module looks like.

00:00:36.904 --> 00:00:39.469
Here's the whole module in one sentence.

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:42.184
Most crypto losses aren't market losses.

00:00:42.184 --> 00:00:45.214
They're mistakes and scams: a seed phrase typed

00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:48.055
into a fake site, an approval signed without

00:00:48.055 --> 00:00:51.449
reading, a transaction sent on the wrong network.

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:54.204
This module builds the habits that prevent them.

00:00:55.504 --> 00:00:58.047
Here's roughly how beginner losses break

00:00:58.047 --> 00:01:01.139
down, as categories, not measured statistics.

00:01:01.139 --> 00:01:03.434
A leaked seed phrase or private key.

00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:06.439
A signed approval that let someone else move funds.

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:07.942
A payment sent on the wrong

00:01:07.942 --> 00:01:09.960
network or to the wrong address.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.054
And outright phishing, a fake site

00:01:12.054 --> 00:01:13.790
or a fake support agent.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.082
Notice what's missing: “the market went

00:01:16.082 --> 00:01:17.815
down” isn't on this list.

00:01:17.815 --> 00:01:20.040
That's what this module is built to close.

00:01:21.341 --> 00:01:23.231
This isn't a small problem.

00:01:23.231 --> 00:01:26.991
Multiple industry trackers have found that wallet-draining

00:01:26.991 --> 00:01:29.159
phishing scams are now one of the

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:32.537
largest single categories of user-level crypto loss,

00:01:32.537 --> 00:01:35.641
year after year, bigger than exchange hacks.

00:01:35.641 --> 00:01:37.966
Not because the technology is weak.

00:01:37.966 --> 00:01:40.071
Because habits are the actual perimeter.

00:01:41.371 --> 00:01:43.146
Now, the five words you'll need.

00:01:44.446 --> 00:01:46.271
Word one: private key.

00:01:46.271 --> 00:01:48.526
The secret that signs transactions.

00:01:48.526 --> 00:01:50.185
You met this in Module Zero,

00:01:50.185 --> 00:01:52.316
but here's the operator version.

00:01:52.316 --> 00:01:54.300
A burner wallet is a wallet with

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.645
only a small, disposable amount in it,

00:01:56.645 --> 00:01:58.568
used to try new apps first.

00:01:58.568 --> 00:02:00.102
Its private key can sign

00:02:00.102 --> 00:02:02.428
transactions, same as any wallet.

00:02:02.428 --> 00:02:04.397
The difference is what's at risk if

00:02:04.397 --> 00:02:07.023
it's ever compromised: a little, not everything.

00:02:08.323 --> 00:02:10.958
One real-world number for the burner wallet.

00:02:10.958 --> 00:02:14.359
Most operators keep somewhere between $20 and $100 in

00:02:14.359 --> 00:02:17.131
a burner, enough to actually use a new app,

00:02:17.131 --> 00:02:20.643
small enough that losing it entirely wouldn't hurt.

00:02:20.643 --> 00:02:22.151
That's the number to aim for while

00:02:22.151 --> 00:02:24.003
you're still learning which apps you trust.

00:02:25.303 --> 00:02:26.988
Word two: approval.

00:02:26.988 --> 00:02:28.618
Permission for an app to move

00:02:28.618 --> 00:02:30.168
a token on your behalf.

00:02:30.168 --> 00:02:33.152
From Lesson one point four: an approval names a

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:36.012
spender, a token, and an amount, and it keeps

00:02:36.012 --> 00:02:38.923
working every time after you sign it once.

00:02:38.923 --> 00:02:40.098
That's the trap.

00:02:40.098 --> 00:02:43.053
An approval outlives the trade it was signed for.

00:02:43.053 --> 00:02:44.878
Revoke the ones you no longer use.

00:02:46.178 --> 00:02:48.888
One habit that comes straight from that anatomy.

00:02:48.888 --> 00:02:52.137
Most apps default to asking for an unlimited approval,

00:02:52.137 --> 00:02:55.505
permission to move any amount, forever, because it means

00:02:55.505 --> 00:02:57.873
you won't be asked again next time.

00:02:57.873 --> 00:03:00.684
A safer habit, where your wallet allows it, is

00:03:00.684 --> 00:03:04.333
an exact approval: just enough for this one transaction.

00:03:04.333 --> 00:03:06.963
It costs you one extra click now and then.

00:03:06.963 --> 00:03:09.133
It also means a compromised app can

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:11.123
only ever take what you approved.

00:03:12.423 --> 00:03:16.098
Word three: multisig, short for multi-signature.

00:03:16.098 --> 00:03:18.496
A wallet that needs several separate keys

00:03:18.496 --> 00:03:20.338
to agree before it can act.

00:03:20.338 --> 00:03:22.503
Picture a vault with three locks, held

00:03:22.503 --> 00:03:24.668
by three different people, and any two

00:03:24.668 --> 00:03:26.633
of them are enough to open it.

00:03:26.633 --> 00:03:29.673
One compromised key isn't enough on its own.

00:03:29.673 --> 00:03:32.598
That's the whole idea: no single point of failure.

00:03:33.898 --> 00:03:36.441
So with a two-of-three multisig, any two

00:03:36.441 --> 00:03:38.673
keys can move funds, but one stolen

00:03:38.673 --> 00:03:40.958
key, on its own, can do nothing.

00:03:40.958 --> 00:03:44.175
That's why serious amounts, later in this program,

00:03:44.175 --> 00:03:46.825
move into a multisig, not a single wallet.

00:03:48.124 --> 00:03:49.809
Word four: phishing.

00:03:49.809 --> 00:03:52.234
A fake site or message built to steal.

00:03:52.234 --> 00:03:55.855
From Lesson one point six: fake look-alike sites, drainers

00:03:55.855 --> 00:03:59.354
that get one signature to grant everything, fake support

00:03:59.354 --> 00:04:03.159
messages asking for your seed, and poisoned addresses slipped

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:06.589
into your history hoping you'll copy the wrong one.

00:04:06.589 --> 00:04:08.561
The pattern is always pressure, plus

00:04:08.561 --> 00:04:10.374
a shortcut around your own checks.

00:04:11.674 --> 00:04:13.599
Word five: simulation.

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:15.652
A preview of what a transaction will

00:04:15.652 --> 00:04:17.949
actually change, before you sign it.

00:04:17.949 --> 00:04:21.072
From Lesson one point seven: a simulator shows you

00:04:21.072 --> 00:04:24.502
the real result, what leaves your wallet, what arrives,

00:04:24.502 --> 00:04:28.354
what approvals get granted, so you're never signing blind.

00:04:28.354 --> 00:04:30.160
If a wallet or tool can show

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.464
you a simulation, use it, every time.

00:04:33.764 --> 00:04:34.972
And here's how to actually

00:04:34.972 --> 00:04:36.569
catch phishing in the moment.

00:04:36.569 --> 00:04:39.066
A fake site copies the real one closely,

00:04:39.066 --> 00:04:41.684
but the web address is slightly wrong, and

00:04:41.684 --> 00:04:44.303
you usually reached it through a search ad

00:04:44.303 --> 00:04:46.369
or a message, not a bookmark.

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:48.897
A real site's address matches exactly, and

00:04:48.897 --> 00:04:51.249
you got there through your own bookmark

00:04:51.249 --> 00:04:53.019
or by typing it yourself.

00:04:53.019 --> 00:04:54.847
A padlock icon in the browser

00:04:54.847 --> 00:04:57.279
proves the connection is encrypted.

00:04:57.279 --> 00:04:59.574
It proves nothing about who's on the other end.

00:05:00.874 --> 00:05:01.964
Quick check.

00:05:01.964 --> 00:05:04.729
What's the core idea behind a multisig wallet?

00:05:09.149 --> 00:05:12.031
The answer: it needs several separate keys to

00:05:12.031 --> 00:05:14.474
agree before it can act, so one stolen

00:05:14.474 --> 00:05:16.854
key, on its own, can't move the funds.

00:05:18.154 --> 00:05:19.199
Before you start.

00:05:20.499 --> 00:05:23.309
Here's a preview of Lesson one point two, because

00:05:23.309 --> 00:05:26.194
it matters for everything else in this module.

00:05:26.194 --> 00:05:29.698
Every transaction you'll ever sign follows this same life

00:05:29.698 --> 00:05:32.597
cycle: you sign it, the network checks it, it's

00:05:32.597 --> 00:05:35.434
added to a block, and it reaches finality.

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:38.922
Every safety habit in this module, exact approvals,

00:05:38.922 --> 00:05:43.149
simulation, multisig, is really about controlling what happens

00:05:43.149 --> 00:05:45.094
at that first step: the sign.

00:05:46.394 --> 00:05:48.814
Two things, both from Module Zero.

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:52.228
First, Module Zero done: a secured exchange account,

00:05:52.228 --> 00:05:55.169
and a wallet whose restore you've tested.

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:58.222
Second, a small amount already sitting in that wallet,

00:05:58.222 --> 00:06:01.019
on a low-fee network, ready to practise with.

00:06:01.019 --> 00:06:02.918
If either is missing, go back

00:06:02.918 --> 00:06:05.054
and finish Module Zero first.

00:06:05.054 --> 00:06:07.769
This module builds directly on those habits.

00:06:09.069 --> 00:06:10.344
Your first safe step.

00:06:11.644 --> 00:06:13.714
Your first step in this module doesn't

00:06:13.714 --> 00:06:15.234
touch a new app at all.

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:18.165
Open your wallet's approvals screen, or a revoke

00:06:18.165 --> 00:06:21.215
tool, list every approval you've ever granted, and

00:06:21.215 --> 00:06:23.609
revoke any you don't currently use.

00:06:23.609 --> 00:06:26.038
It takes about ten minutes, and it closes

00:06:26.038 --> 00:06:28.409
the exact door word two warned you about.

00:06:29.709 --> 00:06:31.394
Here's the worked example.

00:06:31.394 --> 00:06:33.649
Open your wallet's approvals screen.

00:06:33.649 --> 00:06:35.801
Read down the list: which app, which

00:06:35.801 --> 00:06:37.729
token, how much it can move.

00:06:37.729 --> 00:06:40.644
For each one, ask: am I still using this?

00:06:40.644 --> 00:06:42.079
If yes, leave it.

00:06:42.079 --> 00:06:44.414
If no, revoke it, which costs a small

00:06:44.414 --> 00:06:47.909
network fee but permanently removes that permission.

00:06:47.909 --> 00:06:50.489
Ten minutes, and every unused door is closed.

00:06:51.789 --> 00:06:52.879
Quick check.

00:06:52.879 --> 00:06:55.296
Why is an old, unused approval worth

00:06:55.296 --> 00:06:58.104
revoking, even if nothing's gone wrong yet?

00:07:02.524 --> 00:07:05.346
The answer: an approval outlives the trade it was

00:07:05.346 --> 00:07:08.469
signed for, and keeps working until you revoke it.

00:07:08.469 --> 00:07:10.585
If that app is ever compromised, an

00:07:10.585 --> 00:07:12.584
old approval is still a live door.

00:07:13.884 --> 00:07:14.979
The mastery ladder.

00:07:16.279 --> 00:07:19.304
Here's how you'll measure your progress through Module One.

00:07:19.304 --> 00:07:21.671
Beginner: uses a separate burner wallet,

00:07:21.671 --> 00:07:24.164
and reads every prompt before signing.

00:07:24.164 --> 00:07:28.297
Practitioner: runs the before-signing checklist from memory, keeps

00:07:28.297 --> 00:07:31.434
approvals limited, and uses a hardware wallet.

00:07:31.434 --> 00:07:34.976
And Master: a multisig vault, a simulation on every

00:07:34.976 --> 00:07:38.859
signature, private addresses, and a tested recovery plan.

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:42.759
Here's the road ahead, lesson by lesson.

00:07:42.759 --> 00:07:46.639
1.1, what DeFi is, and the risk-first mindset.

00:07:46.639 --> 00:07:50.075
1.2, how a transaction actually happens.

00:07:50.075 --> 00:07:53.855
1.3, wallets, keys, hardware and multisig.

00:07:53.855 --> 00:07:57.504
1.4, tokens, approvals and allowances.

00:07:57.504 --> 00:08:00.889
1.5, stablecoins and how they break.

00:08:00.889 --> 00:08:03.149
1.6, scam defence.

00:08:03.149 --> 00:08:07.439
1.7, reading signatures and simulating transactions.

00:08:07.439 --> 00:08:10.644
1.8, privacy and physical security.

00:08:10.644 --> 00:08:14.004
And 1.9, smart accounts and account abstraction.

00:08:15.304 --> 00:08:18.189
One more preview, from Lesson one point eight.

00:08:18.189 --> 00:08:21.636
Your wallet address isn't your identity, until you connect

00:08:21.636 --> 00:08:24.266
the two, by posting it publicly next to your

00:08:24.266 --> 00:08:27.129
name, or letting a delivery address and a crypto

00:08:27.129 --> 00:08:29.474
balance touch the same paper trail.

00:08:29.474 --> 00:08:32.181
Privacy and physical security round out this

00:08:32.181 --> 00:08:34.767
module for exactly that reason: the safest

00:08:34.767 --> 00:08:37.113
signature in the world doesn't help if

00:08:37.113 --> 00:08:39.339
someone knows which door to knock on.

00:08:40.639 --> 00:08:43.603
You've mastered this module when you can take a

00:08:43.603 --> 00:08:47.124
brand-new app from first visit, to a limited, simulated,

00:08:47.124 --> 00:08:50.944
verified transaction, and revoke that approval afterward.

00:08:50.944 --> 00:08:52.459
Not a big balance.

00:08:52.459 --> 00:08:54.424
A repeatable, careful process.

00:08:55.724 --> 00:08:56.764
One more.

00:08:56.764 --> 00:08:59.804
You get a message from “support,” claiming your wallet

00:08:59.804 --> 00:09:02.149
has a problem, with a link to fix it.

00:09:02.149 --> 00:09:04.183
The web address looks almost right,

00:09:04.183 --> 00:09:06.009
and there's a padlock icon.

00:09:06.009 --> 00:09:06.924
What should you do?

00:09:11.344 --> 00:09:13.129
The answer: don't click it.

00:09:13.129 --> 00:09:17.099
Nobody legitimate messages you first, the address is “almost

00:09:17.099 --> 00:09:20.224
right,” not right, and a padlock only means the

00:09:20.224 --> 00:09:23.999
connection is encrypted, not that the destination is real.

00:09:25.299 --> 00:09:26.484
Last check.

00:09:26.484 --> 00:09:29.369
What does mastering Module One actually look like?

00:09:33.789 --> 00:09:36.013
The answer: taking a new app from

00:09:36.013 --> 00:09:39.023
first visit to a limited, simulated, verified

00:09:39.023 --> 00:09:42.229
transaction, and revoking the approval afterward.

00:09:43.529 --> 00:09:45.484
Let's recap the five words.

00:09:45.484 --> 00:09:48.080
Private key: the secret that signs, kept small

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:50.644
in a burner wallet while you're learning.

00:09:50.644 --> 00:09:53.189
Approval: permission that outlives the trade,

00:09:53.189 --> 00:09:55.094
so revoke what you don't use.

00:09:55.094 --> 00:09:58.804
Multisig: several keys, no single point of failure.

00:09:58.804 --> 00:10:01.929
Phishing: pressure plus a shortcut around your checks.

00:10:01.929 --> 00:10:04.294
And simulation: never sign blind.

00:10:05.594 --> 00:10:07.289
That's the Mastery Starter.

00:10:07.289 --> 00:10:09.108
You now have the whole module in

00:10:09.108 --> 00:10:11.259
your head: why losses happen, the five

00:10:11.259 --> 00:10:13.299
words, your first safe step, and the

00:10:13.299 --> 00:10:15.749
ladder that tells you when you're done.

00:10:15.749 --> 00:10:18.149
Next up, Lesson 1.1: What DeFi

00:10:18.149 --> 00:10:20.549
is, and the risk-first mindset.
